# Domain Configuration (Production)
VITE_FRONTEND_DOMAIN=orewire.com
VITE_BACKEND_DOMAIN=backend.orewire.com

# API URL — REQUIRED for production builds (see below)
VITE_API_URL=https://backend.orewire.com/api

# ── Dokploy / Docker ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# VITE_* values are baked in at BUILD time (npm run build), NOT at container runtime.
# In Dokploy → frontend app → Build → set **Build Arguments** (not only Environment):
#
#   VITE_API_URL=https://backend.orewire.com/api
#   VITE_FRONTEND_DOMAIN=orewire.com
#   VITE_BACKEND_DOMAIN=backend.orewire.com
#
# If VITE_API_URL is omitted but VITE_BACKEND_DOMAIN is set at build time,
# the app derives https://{VITE_BACKEND_DOMAIN}/api automatically.
#
# After changing any VITE_* value, rebuild and redeploy the frontend image.

# Google Analytics 4 (optional override; defaults to G-W5EY5J3B1W)
# Loaded only after the visitor clicks Accept all on the cookie banner.
# VITE_GA_MEASUREMENT_ID=G-W5EY5J3B1W

# ── Crawler rendering (RUNTIME, not build time) ──────────────────────────────
# This one is different from every VITE_* value above: it is read by nginx when
# the container STARTS, not by npm run build, so set it as a plain Environment
# variable and a restart is enough — no rebuild required.
#
# nginx sends crawler requests (Googlebot, GPTBot, PerplexityBot, ClaudeBot and
# friends) for /company, /companies, /news and /filings to this backend, and
# serves /sitemap.xml, /sitemaps/*.xml and /llms.txt from it. Everyone else keeps
# getting the React app.
#
# If this is unset or unreachable, the site still works perfectly in a browser
# while being invisible to crawlers — which is the exact bug this fixes. The
# Dockerfile defaults it to the public backend host; an internal service URL is
# faster. Verify with: cd Server && npm run seo:audit
#
# BACKEND_UPSTREAM=http://backend.orewire.com

# ── New crawlers (RUNTIME) ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The crawler list in nginx.conf covers the search engines, AI answer engines and
# social unfurlers that exist today. New ones appear regularly, and when one is
# missing the failure is silent: that bot gets the empty SPA shell, the site
# looks perfect in a browser, and nothing reports a problem.
#
# To add one without editing nginx.conf or rebuilding the image, set this to an
# nginx map mask — it MUST include its own match operator:
#
# EXTRA_CRAWLER_UA_PATTERN=~*newai-bot
#
# Defaults to `~*orewire-never-matches`, a valid regex matching no real
# User-Agent, so the map always has a valid mask. If the leading `~*` is omitted
# the value becomes an exact match, which will not match a real User-Agent: it
# fails safe, serving the SPA and never the reverse.
